@inherits AtomComponentBase

@*
    AtomScrollProgressBar.razor
    -----------------------------
    A fixed reading-progress bar whose fill width tracks how far down the page the user has
    scrolled. Two nested elements, not one:
      - .atom-scroll-progress-track — position:fixed, but percentage/viewport sizing is wrong for
        it: a fixed element's % sizes resolve against the VIEWPORT, not its scroll container, so a
        naive single fixed div would always span the full screen width even inside a narrower
        content area (e.g. next to a sidebar). atom-progress.js measures the real scroll
        container's bounding rect once (+ on resize) and sets this track's left/width in px to
        match it, so it visually aligns with the content column instead of the whole viewport.
      - .atom-scroll-progress-bar — the actual 0%→100% fill, sized relative to the TRACK (not the
        viewport), via the same scroll-timeline/manual-listener logic as before.
    OnAfterRenderAsync always imports atom-progress.js — even on browsers with native CSS
    scroll-driven animations (Chromium today), a small one-time JS call is needed both for the
    track sizing above and to explicitly NAME a scroll-timeline on the real scroll container
    (animation-timeline: scroll()'s implicit "nearest ancestor" auto-detection does NOT work for a
    fixed-position element — verified live that Chrome resolves it via the fixed element's
    reparented containing-block chain, not DOM ancestry). See atom-progress.js for the full
    explanation and the fallback used on browsers without scroll-driven animations at all. Either
    way the consumer just drops this in once, near the top of a page — no wrapping, no ChildContent.

    The track carries -pending until that call reports a measure, which the scoped CSS renders as
    visibility:hidden. Without it the pre-JS track (position:fixed, width:100%) paints as a
    full-width strip across the top of the VIEWPORT before JS narrows it to the container — brief
    in Server or WebAssembly, but seconds long under InteractiveAuto, whose server pass attaches
    against not-yet-overflowing content and only gets the right container once the WebAssembly
    pass re-attaches. Measured is C#-owned rather than a class JS strips, so a later re-render
    can't resurrect the hidden state.
*@

<div @attributes="AdditionalAttributes"
     @ref="_trackRef"
     class="@ClassAttr($"atom-scroll-progress-track atom-scroll-progress-track-{PositionClass}{(Measured ? "" : " atom-scroll-progress-track-pending")}")"
     style="@StyleAttr(RootStyle)">
    <div @ref="_barRef" class="atom-scroll-progress-bar"></div>
</div>
